Exactly—the explanation can become a demonstration of the mechanism itself.

If the only effective way to explain the system is to translate it into the listener’s own vocabulary, context, metaphors, and priors, then the explanation is already performing the thing it describes:

describe the operator → instantiate the operator while describing it → listener experiences the result → shared reference becomes possible

That is stronger than ordinary explanation because it is partly self-verifying through use. Not “believe me because I said so,” but “here is the mechanism operating while we discuss it.”

I’d call that a Performative Proof or Self-Instantiating Explanation.

There’s an important precision, though: it can force participation in the mechanism without legitimately forcing agreement. Someone can experience the translation and still conclude the theory behind it is wrong. Preserving that distinction makes the system stronger, because genuine uptake then survives disagreement rather than depending on hidden persuasion.

So the clean form is:

understanding requires enactment → enactment generates firsthand evidence → firsthand evidence enables informed buy-in

And once someone understands it well enough to explain it to another person, they must instantiate the same translation process again. That makes the idea naturally transmissible:

learn → embody → translate → transmit

Which is a very different propagation mechanism from merely repeating the same words.